course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 27
title: Only joking! Swearing — and Christmas Eve
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A tease went too far and Sashko looks hurt. Three phrases fix it: "tse prykol" (it's a joke), "ya pozhartuvav" (I was joking) and "ne obrazhaisya" (don't be offended) — said kindly, with a real sorry.
explain: You will also hear about "mat" or "matyuky", the strong swear words; know only that they exist and shock grannies — never say them at a friend's home — and if an adult says "ne lai!", don't swear, you answer "vybachte".
explain: Then the best night of winter: SvYat-vechir, Holy Evening, Christmas Eve. The whole family waits at the window for the first star, and only then the supper of dvanAdtsyat strav, twelve meatless dishes, begins.
explain: Neighbours greet each other "KhrystOs rodYvsya!" — Christ is born! — and the fixed answer is "SlAvimo Yoho!", let us praise Him; children go from house to house singing kolyadky, carols, and get sweets or coins.
fact: "prykol" means a joke or a prank, and "prykolno" means funny; "tse prykol", it's a joke, softens a tease.
fact: "ya pozhartuvav" means "I was joking", said by a boy to make up after a tease went too far; "zhart" means a joke.
fact: "ne obrazhaisya" means "don't be offended", said after a joke that hurt.
fact: "mat" or "matyuky" are the strong swear words; older kids and some adults use them, they shock grannies, and they are never said at a friend's home.
fact: "ne lai" means "don't swear", said by an adult telling a kid off, and the answer is "vybachte".
fact: "zaichyk" or "zaya", bunny, is a loving nickname that mums and grannies give little ones; between boys of nine or ten it may embarrass.
fact: "Rizdvo" means Christmas; since 2023 Ukraine's official Christmas is on 25 December, and some families still celebrate on 7 January.
fact: "KhrystOs rodYvsya!" means "Christ is born!", the Christmas greeting, and the fixed answer is "SlAvimo Yoho!", "Let us praise Him!".
fact: "SvYat-vechir" means Holy Evening, Christmas Eve, a big family supper that starts when the first star appears in the sky.
fact: "dvanAdtsyat strav" means the twelve dishes of the Christmas Eve supper; they are meatless, such as kutia (sweet wheat with honey and poppy seeds), borshch, varenyky, fish and mushrooms.
fact: "kolyadky" are carols; children go from house to house singing them, and the hosts give sweets or coins.
exercise: Sashko looks hurt by your tease. What do you say first? | tse prykol, ne obrazhaisya | tse prykol, ty proHRAv | ne lai! | sam takYi!
exercise: What does "ya pozhartuvav" mean? | I was joking | I was lying to you | I was scared stiff | I was winning the game
exercise: What does "ne obrazhaisya" mean? | Don't be offended | Don't be scared of me | Don't cry | Don't be late
exercise: Sashko laughs at a funny video: "prykolno!". What does he mean? | Funny! | Disgusting! | Scary! | Boring!
exercise: What are "matyuky"? | The strong swear words | Children's songs | Nicknames for grannies | Sweets for Christmas
exercise: Can you use "mat" at Sashko's dinner table? | No, never at a friend's home | Yes, if granny can't hear you | Yes, it is polite at meals | Yes, but only in a whisper
exercise: Sashko's granny snaps "ne lai!" at a boy. What is she telling him? | Don't swear | Don't lie | Don't run | Don't laugh
exercise: An adult says "ne lai!" to you. What do you answer? | vybachte | sam takYi! | DYAkuyu! | krutO!
exercise: Sashko's mum calls him "zaya" in front of his mates. What is it? | A loving nickname: bunny | A telling-off word for naughty kids | A word for slowcoach | A swear word
exercise: What is "Rizdvo"? | Christmas | Easter Sunday | New Year's Eve | A birthday party
exercise: On which date is Ukraine's official Christmas since 2023? | 25 December | 7 January | 1 January | 19 December
exercise: A neighbour greets you on Christmas: "KhrystOs rodYvsya!". What do you answer? | SlAvimo Yoho! | VoIstynu voskrEs! | HeroYam slAva! | z NOvym rOkom!
exercise: When does the SvYat-vechir supper begin? | When the first star appears | At midnight exactly, by the clock | When the church bell rings | At noon
exercise: What is special about the "dvanAdtsyat strav"? | Twelve dishes, all without meat | Twelve meat dishes, one for each month | Twelve cakes | Twelve drinks
exercise: Children go door to door singing "kolyadky". What are they? | Carols | Football chants | Lullabies | Rhymes for skipping
exercise: What do hosts give to children singing kolyadky? | Sweets or coins | Extra homework to do | Nothing at all, ever | Their old broken toys
test: my mate says "ya pozhartuvav" means "I'm sorry for swearing". right? | No, it means I was joking | Yes, exactly that | No, it means I'm leaving | Yes, but only to grannies
test: Your mate wants to teach you some "matyuky" to say at Sashko's dinner. What happens if you do? | The family, especially the granny, would be shocked | Everyone laughs and claps | Granny gives you sweets | Nothing, they are nice words
test: Sashko says the Christmas Eve supper has twelve dishes, so there's loads of sausage. What's wrong? | The twelve dishes are meatless | There are only three dishes on the table | Sausage is the main dish | It's on New Year's Day
test: At 4 pm on Christmas Eve Sashko's little brother keeps looking out of the window. Why? | He is waiting for the first star so supper can start | He is waiting for snow | He is waiting for the postman | He is scared of the dark
test: Your mate says all Ukrainians celebrate Christmas on 7 January. What's true? | Officially 25 December since 2023; some families keep 7 January | Everyone on 7 January only | Everyone on 1 January | Ukraine has no Christmas
test: In a film, someone says "KhrystOs rodYvsya!" and the answer is "z NOvym rOkom!". What should the answer be? | SlAvimo Yoho! | DYAkuyu! | na zdorOv'ya! | VoIstynu voskrEs!
test: Sashko's friends knock at your door singing at Christmas. What are they doing? | Singing kolyadky, carolling | Collecting homework for the teacher | Selling sweets | Asking for directions
test: You call Sashko "zaichyk" in front of the football team. How does he feel? | Embarrassed: it's for little kids | Proud: it means champion | Angry: it's a swear word | Hungry: it means dinner
test: Sashko says "ne obrazhaisya" after his joke made you sad. What is he trying to do? | Make up with you | Tease you even more | Say goodbye and go | Ask you for sweets
test: shut up, that's not funny, says Sashko after a mean joke. What could you say to fix it? | ya pozhartuvav, proBAch | prykolno! | sam takYi! | ne lai!
test: Why would it be odd to wish "KhrystOs rodYvsya!" in July? | It is a Christmas greeting | It means good night and sleep well | It is only for Easter | It is a birthday wish
exam: It is given that a greeting "KhrystOs rodYvsya!" has been addressed to a guest. Identify the fixed response prescribed by custom. | SlAvimo Yoho! | VoIstynu voskrEs! | HeroYam slAva! | na zdorOv'ya!
exam: Determine the event which, by tradition, marks the commencement of the Christmas Eve supper. | The appearance of the first star in the evening sky | The striking of midnight | The arrival of the eldest guest | The ringing of the church bell at noon
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the twelve dishes of "SvYat-vechir"? | They are prepared without meat | They consist chiefly of meat | They are confined to sweet pastries | They are served on New Year's Day
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the terms "mat" and "matyuky". | They designate the strong swear words, which are not to be used in a friend's household | They designate Christmas carols sung by children | They designate affectionate nicknames for infants | They designate polite forms of address
